LM.SEARCH_INDEX = {"version":1,"records":[{"id":"page:a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation","group":"pages","title":"Alphabet, sounds and stress","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Polish letters and sounds, the letters with marks, and the stress rule: second-to-last syllable. Dzień dobry. Dziękuję.","text":"Polish spelling is far more regular than English: know the letters and you can read any word aloud. There are no silent letters, and the stress almost always falls on the second-to-last syllable. New for English speakers: nine letters with marks (ą, ć, ę, ł, ń, ó, ś, ź, ż) and letter pairs that make one sound (sz, cz, rz, ch, dz, dż). How it works 32 letters (q, v, x only in foreign words): a ą b c ć d e ę f g h i j k l ł m n ń o ó p r s ś t u w y z ź ż Letters that surprise English speakers: Letter Sounds like Example c \"ts\" in cats noc (night) ch, h \"ch\" in Scottish loch chleb (bread), herbata (tea) cz \"ch\" in church czas (time) sz \"sh\" in shop szkoła (school) rz, ż \"s\" in pleasure rzeka (river), żona (wife) j \"y\" in yes jajko (egg) w \"v\" in van woda (water) ł \"w\" in water mały (small) y \"i\" in bit ty (you) ó, u \"oo\" in book ołówek (pencil), ucho (ear) ą nasal \"on\", as in French bon są (they are) ę nasal \"en\"; at the end of a word almost a plain \"e\" ręka (hand), dziękuję (thank you) Soft sounds have two spellings: a mark before a consonant or at the end of a word, an i before a vowel. If the vowel is i itself, just write it: zima (winter), nic (nothing). Mark Before a vowel Sounds like Example ś si a soft \"sh\" (tongue flat, as in sheep) środa, siostra ć ci a soft \"ch\" (as in cheese) cześć, ciocia ź zi a soft \"zh\" źle, zima dź dzi a soft \"j\" (as in jeep) dzień ń ni \"ny\" in canyon koń, nie Stress: the second-to-last syllable. An i before a vowel is not a syllable of its own: Ka-sia has two. When to use it Reading a new word: say every letter or letter pair; an i before a vowel only softens the sound before it. Kraków = Kra-ków. Stress: count syllables from the end. War-sza-wa → war-SZA-wa (Warsaw). Marks change words: sad (orchard) and sąd (court) are different words. rz / ż, ó / u and ch / h sound the same, so learn each word with its spelling. Examples Polish English Stress Dzień dobry. Good morning. / Hello. dzień DO-bry Dziękuję. Thank you. dzię-KU-ję Przepraszam. Excuse me. / Sorry. prze-PRA-szam Warszawa Warsaw war-SZA-wa herbata tea her-BA-ta poniedziałek Monday po-nie-DZIA-łek Good to know At the end of a word b, d, g, w, z sound like p, t, k, f, s: chleb sounds like \"chlep\", Kraków like \"Krakuf\". After p, t, k, ch the pair rz sounds like sz: przepraszam, trzy (three). Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Lodz → ✓ Łódź — Lodz is the English spelling; in Polish the marks are part of the letters. ✗ woda said with an English \"w\" → ✓ woda with a \"v\" — Polish w is a v sound (an f at the end of a word). ✗ WAR-sza-wa → ✓ war-SZA-wa — stress the second-to-last syllable, not the first. ✗ rzeka said as \"r\" + \"z\" → ✓ rzeka with the \"s\" of pleasure — rz is one sound, the same as ż. Practice Write the stressed syllable in capitals. te-le-fon her-ba-ta ro-wer Which spelling makes the same sound? ż sounds the same as . ó sounds the same as . Add the missing marks. Dzien dobry. Przepraszam, ktora godzina? (Excuse me, what time is it?) How do you say it? The w in woda sounds like English . Answers te-LE-fon her-BA-ta RO-wer rz u Dzień dobry. Przepraszam, która godzina? v","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-alphabet-pronunciation"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-greetings-introductions","group":"pages","title":"Greet people, say goodbye and introduce yourself","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Dzień dobry or Cześć? Formal and informal greetings, saying your name, Miło mi, and ways to say goodbye.","text":"English uses \"hello\" with almost everyone. Polish makes a clear choice: Dzień dobry (formal) for strangers, shops, offices and older people, Cześć (informal) for friends, family and children. Choosing the wrong one is the first thing people notice, so learn the pairs together. To say your name, Polish does not say \"my name is\": it says Mam na imię Anna (\"I have the name Anna\") or simply Jestem Anna. Key phrases Saying hello Polish English Dzień dobry. Good morning. / Good afternoon. / Hello. (formal, until evening) Dobry wieczór. Good evening. (formal) Cześć! Hi! (informal) Hej! Hey! (informal) Saying who you are Polish English Mam na imię Anna. My name is Anna. (first name) Nazywam się Anna Nowak. My name is Anna Nowak. (full name, formal) Jestem Tom. I'm Tom. Jak masz na imię? What's your name? (informal) Jak się pan nazywa? / Jak się pani nazywa? What's your name? (formal, to a man / to a woman) Miło mi. / Bardzo mi miło. Nice to meet you. Asking how someone is Polish English Jak się masz? How are you? (informal) Jak się pan ma? / Jak się pani ma? How are you? (formal, to a man / to a woman) Dobrze, dziękuję. A ty? Fine, thanks. And you? (informal) Dobrze, dziękuję. A pan? / A pani? Fine, thank you. And you? (formal) W porządku. All right. / OK. Saying goodbye Polish English Do widzenia. Goodbye. (formal) Cześć! / Na razie! Bye! / See you! (informal) Do zobaczenia. See you. (neutral, also formal) Do jutra! See you tomorrow! Dobranoc. Good night. (late, when you leave or go to bed) Cześć works both ways: hello and goodbye. Dialogue Anna meets her neighbour, pan Adam, outside their block of flats. Her friend Piotr arrives. Anna: Dzień dobry! (Hello!) Pan Adam: Dzień dobry! Jak się pani ma? (Hello! How are you?) Anna: Dobrze, dziękuję. A pan? (Fine, thank you. And you?) Pan Adam: W porządku, dziękuję. (All right, thank you.) Piotr: Cześć, Ania! (Hi, Ania!) Anna: Cześć, Piotrek! To jest pan Adam Nowak. (Hi, Piotrek! This is Mr Adam Nowak.) Piotr: Dzień dobry. Nazywam się Piotr Wójcik. Miło mi. (Hello. My name is Piotr Wójcik. Nice to meet you.) Pan Adam: Bardzo mi miło. Do widzenia! (Very nice to meet you. Goodbye!) Anna: Do widzenia! (Goodbye!) Anna and Piotr are friends, so they say Cześć. With pan Adam both use Dzień dobry and Do widzenia. Culture note Say Dzień dobry when you walk into a small shop, a doctor's waiting room or a lift, and Do widzenia when you leave. Neighbours and colleagues often use pan or pani with a first name: pan Adam, pani Ewa. It is polite but friendly. Friends use short forms of names: Anna → Ania, Anka; Piotr → Piotrek; Katarzyna → Kasia. People usually shake hands when they meet for the first time. Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Dobry dzień! → ✓ Dzień dobry! — a fixed phrase; the word order is not the English \"good day\". ✗ Moje imię jest Tom. → ✓ Mam na imię Tom. — Polish says \"I have the name\", or just Jestem Tom. ✗ Jak się jesteś? → ✓ Jak się masz? — the phrase uses masz (\"have\"), not \"are\". ✗ Cześć! to a shop assistant → ✓ Dzień dobry! — cześć is for friends, family and people your age in informal settings. ✗ Dziekuje. → ✓ Dziękuję. — the marks are part of the spelling. Practice Formal or informal? Choose the right greeting. You meet your friend Kasia: Dzień dobry or Cześć? You walk into a bakery: Dzień dobry or Cześć? You leave a doctor's office: Do widzenia or Na razie? Complete the phrase. Mam imię Marek. (My name is Marek.) Bardzo mi . (Very nice to meet you.) Translate into Polish. How are you? (to a friend) How are you? (formal, to a woman) Good evening. (formal) Answers Cześć Dzień dobry Do widzenia na miło Jak się masz? Jak się pani ma? Dobry wieczór.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-greetings-introductions"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-pronouns-pan-pani","group":"pages","title":"Personal pronouns and formal address","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"ja, ty, on, ona, ono, my, wy, oni, one, and the polite pan, pani, państwo for \"you\". Jak się pan ma?","text":"English has one \"you\" for everyone. Polish has ty (one person) and wy (several people) for friends, family and children, and pan (to a man), pani (to a woman), państwo (to a group) for everyone else. The polite words really mean \"sir\", \"madam\" and \"ladies and gentlemen\", so the verb after them is the \"he / she / they\" form. Polish also has two words for \"they\", and often leaves pronouns out. How it works Person Singular Plural 1st ja (I) my (we) 2nd, informal ty (you, one friend) wy (you, several friends) 3rd on (he), ona (she), ono (it) oni (they: a group with at least one man), one (they: all other groups) Formal \"you\": Talking to Polish Verb form a man pan like on: Jak się pan ma? a woman pani like ona: Jak się pani ma? a mixed group, or a couple państwo like oni: Jak się państwo mają? a group of men / of women panowie / panie like oni / one ono is for neuter nouns, for example dziecko (child). Things are usually on or ona: a phone (telefon) is on, a lamp (lampa) is ona: see The three genders of nouns . When to use it Usually leave the pronoun out. The verb ending already says who: Jak się masz? (How are you?), not Jak się ty masz? Use it for contrast or emphasis. Ja jestem Ewa, a ty? (I'm Ewa, and you?) Use ty with friends, family and children. Cześć, Ola! Jak się masz? (Hi, Ola! How are you?) Use pan / pani with strangers, in shops, at work and with older people. Dzień dobry. Jak się pani ma? (Hello. How are you, madam?) Use oni if the group has at least one man, one if it has none. Ewa i Marek? Oni są tu. (Ewa and Marek? They're here.) Examples Polish English Jak się pan ma? How are you? (to a man) Dobrze, dziękuję. A pani? Fine, thank you. And you? (to a woman) Dzień dobry! Jak się państwo mają? Hello! How are you? (formal, to a couple or a group) Cześć! Jak się macie? Hi! How are you? (to several friends) Kasia? Ona jest tu. Kasia? She's here. Ania i Ola? One są tam. Ania and Ola? They're over there. Piotr i Ewa? Oni są w domu. Piotr and Ewa? They're at home. My jesteśmy z Gdańska, a wy? We're from Gdańsk, and you? jest (is) and są (are) come from być (to be): być (to be) and to jest / to są . Good to know Proszę pana / proszę pani is how you politely get someone's attention: \"Excuse me, sir / madam\". Moving from pan / pani to ty is a social step; usually the older person offers it. Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Jak się pan masz? → ✓ Jak się pan ma? — pan takes the \"he\" form of the verb, not the \"you\" form. ✗ Jak się masz? to a stranger → ✓ Jak się pan ma? / Jak się pani ma? — ty to strangers sounds rude or too familiar. ✗ Jak się macie? to one older person → ✓ Jak się pani ma? — wy is never the polite form for one person. ✗ Anna i Ewa? Oni są tu. → ✓ Anna i Ewa? One są tu. — oni needs at least one man in the group. ✗ Ja jestem Tom. Ja jestem tu. → ✓ Jestem Tom. Jestem tu. — the verb shows the person; repeating ja sounds heavy. Practice Which pronoun? Replace the name or names. Marek → Kasia i Ola → Kasia i Marek → dziecko → Formal or informal? Choose. To a friend: A ty? or A pan? To an older woman you don't know: Jak się masz? or Jak się pani ma? Translate into Polish. How are you? (to a group of friends) And you? (formal, to a man) Answers on one oni ono A ty? Jak się pani ma? Jak się macie? A pan?","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-pronouns-pan-pani"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-byc","group":"pages","title":"być (to be) and to jest / to są","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The forms of być (jestem, jesteś, jest, jesteśmy, jesteście, są) and pointing at things with to jest and to są.","text":"Być is \"to be\". Like English \"am / is / are\", it is irregular, so learn its six forms by heart. Two things are different from English: the ending already says who, so Jestem Tom is a full sentence without \"I\", and \"this is / these are\" is to jest / to są, where to never changes, whatever you point at. How it works Present tense of być: Person Singular Plural 1st (ja) jestem (I am) (my) jesteśmy (we are) 2nd (ty) jesteś (you are) (wy) jesteście (you are) 3rd (on, ona, ono) jest (he / she / it is) (oni, one) są (they are) formal pan / pani jest państwo są Pointing: Polish English Rule To jest Marek. This is Marek. one person or thing: to jest To są moje dzieci. These are my children. more than one: to są Kto to jest? Who is this? people and animals Co to jest? What is this? things Negative: put nie in front: nie jestem, to nie jest. When to use it Saying who you are: Jestem Ola. (I'm Ola.) Describing: Jesteś bardzo miły. (You're very kind. — to a man; to a woman: miła.) Saying where someone is: Gdzie jesteś? — Jestem tu. (Where are you? — I'm here.) Introducing and pointing: To jest pani Nowak. (This is Mrs Nowak.) To są moje siostry. (These are my sisters.) Asking: Czy to jest twój telefon? (Is this your phone?) — czy at the start turns a statement into a yes / no question. Examples Polish English Jestem Kuba, a to jest Zosia. I'm Kuba, and this is Zosia. Gdzie jesteście? — Jesteśmy tu! Where are you? — We're here! Pan jest bardzo miły. You are very kind. (to a man) Kasia i Marek są tam. Kasia and Marek are over there. Co to jest? — To jest herbata. What's this? — It's tea. To są moje dzieci, Antek i Zuzia. These are my children, Antek and Zuzia. Nie jestem zmęczona. I'm not tired. (a woman speaking) Kto to jest? — To jest mój brat. Who's this? — It's my brother. Good to know The adjective after jestem shows your gender: a man says jestem zmęczony, a woman jestem zmęczona. For a job or nationality (I'm a student, she's a doctor) the noun needs a new ending after a form of być (jestem, jest…): Instrumental after być and with z . After to jest the noun does not change: To jest student. Age uses mieć (to have), not być: Mam trzydzieści lat (I'm thirty). See Give and ask for personal details . Tu and tutaj both mean \"here\"; tam is \"there\". Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Oni jest tu. → ✓ Oni są tu. — the plural \"they are\" is są. ✗ To są mój brat. → ✓ To jest mój brat. — one person: to jest. ✗ To jest moje dzieci. → ✓ To są moje dzieci. — dzieci is plural, so to są. ✗ Jestem trzydzieści. → ✓ Mam trzydzieści lat. — age uses mieć, not być. ✗ Ja jestem Tom, ja jestem tu. → ✓ Jestem Tom, jestem tu. — jestem already means \"I am\". Practice Complete with the right form of być. My tu. Ty Ola? Oni w domu. Pan bardzo miły. Wy tam? To jest or to są? moja siostra. moje dzieci. Translate into Polish. Where are you? (to one friend) Answers jesteśmy jesteś są jest jesteście To jest To są Gdzie jesteś?","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: aspect and tenses","route":"#/a1/verbs-byc"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-numbers-days-months","group":"pages","title":"Numbers 0–100, days of the week, months","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Count from zero to a hundred, name the days and months, and say \"on Monday\" and \"in July\". Days and months take small letters.","text":"Polish numbers are built like English ones: dwadzieścia (twenty) + dwa (two) = dwadzieścia dwa (twenty-two), with no \"and\" and no hyphen. Two differences matter from day one: days and months are written with a small letter, and \"on Monday\" or \"in July\" change the end of the word: poniedziałek → w poniedziałek, lipiec → w lipcu. Learn these short phrases as they are; the rules behind them come later. Words Numbers 0–20 Polish English zero 0 jeden 1 dwa 2 trzy 3 cztery 4 pięć 5 sześć 6 siedem 7 osiem 8 dziewięć 9 dziesięć 10 jedenaście 11 dwanaście 12 trzynaście 13 czternaście 14 piętnaście 15 szesnaście 16 siedemnaście 17 osiemnaście 18 dziewiętnaście 19 dwadzieścia 20 Tens and a hundred Polish English trzydzieści 30 czterdzieści 40 pięćdziesiąt 50 sześćdziesiąt 60 siedemdziesiąt 70 osiemdziesiąt 80 dziewięćdziesiąt 90 sto 100 Days of the week Polish English on … poniedziałek (m) Monday w poniedziałek wtorek (m) Tuesday we wtorek środa (f) Wednesday w środę czwartek (m) Thursday w czwartek piątek (m) Friday w piątek sobota (f) Saturday w sobotę niedziela (f) Sunday w niedzielę Months Polish English in … styczeń (m) January w styczniu luty (m) February w lutym marzec (m) March w marcu kwiecień (m) April w kwietniu maj (m) May w maju czerwiec (m) June w czerwcu lipiec (m) July w lipcu sierpień (m) August w sierpniu wrzesień (m) September we wrześniu październik (m) October w październiku listopad (m) November w listopadzie grudzień (m) December w grudniu Word partners Polish English Jaki dzisiaj jest dzień? What day is it today? Dzisiaj jest środa. Today is Wednesday. Jutro jest sobota. Tomorrow is Saturday. w sobotę i w niedzielę on Saturday and Sunday w weekend at the weekend we wtorek rano on Tuesday morning w lipcu i w sierpniu in July and August Mój numer to sześć, zero, jeden… My number is six, zero, one… autobus numer dwadzieścia trzy bus number twenty-three pokój numer czternaście room fourteen Ile? — Dwanaście. How many? — Twelve. Good to know Phone numbers can be read digit by digit: sześć, dwa, zero… Poles often read them in groups of three, which needs the hundreds (A2). we instead of w before w or f followed by another consonant: we wtorek, we wrześniu. It is easier to say. Jeden changes with gender (jedna kawa, jedno piwo), and dwa becomes dwie with feminine nouns (dwie kawy). Counting things comes in Order in a café and buy things . Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ w Poniedziałek → ✓ w poniedziałek — days and months take a small letter in Polish. ✗ w sobota → ✓ w sobotę — \"on\" + a day changes the ending: -a → -ę. ✗ pięćnaście → ✓ piętnaście — 15 and 19 are spelled with t: piętnaście, dziewiętnaście. ✗ dwadzieścia i dwa → ✓ dwadzieścia dwa — no \"and\" inside a number. ✗ w lipiec → ✓ w lipcu — \"in\" + a month changes the ending; learn the w … forms as phrases. Practice Write the numbers in words. 17 45 99 Which day comes next? poniedziałek, wtorek, piątek, sobota, Complete with the right phrase. \"on Saturday\" = w \"in May\" = w Translate into Polish. Today is Thursday. Answers siedemnaście czterdzieści pięć dziewięćdziesiąt dziewięć środa niedziela sobotę maju Dzisiaj jest czwartek. / Dziś jest czwartek.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-numbers-days-months"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-gender-of-nouns","group":"pages","title":"The three genders of nouns","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Every Polish noun is masculine, feminine or neuter, and the last letter usually tells you which. ten dom, ta kawa, to okno.","text":"In English only people and animals have a gender (he, she), and a table is just \"it\". In Polish every noun is masculine, feminine or neuter, even a table or a cup of coffee. Gender decides the form of every adjective and pronoun next to the noun, so it is the first thing to check. The good news: the last letter of the dictionary form tells you the gender in most cases. How it works Gender Usual ending \"this\" Examples masculine (m) a consonant ten ten dom (house), ten telefon, ten kot (cat) feminine (f) -a ta ta kawa (coffee), ta lampa, ta książka (book) neuter (n) -o, -e, -ę, -um to to okno (window), to morze (sea), to imię (name), to muzeum Exceptions worth learning now: Group Gender Examples men's words ending in -a masculine ten tata (dad), ten kolega (friend), ten mężczyzna (man) some nouns ending in a consonant feminine ta noc (night), ta rzecz (thing), ta sól (salt) words ending in -i for women feminine ta pani (lady) dziecko neuter, though it is a person to dziecko (child) Learn these with ta: ta miłość (love), ta jesień (autumn). When to use it Choosing \"this\": ten, ta or to agrees with the noun. Ten pokój jest duży. (This room is big.) Choosing \"he / she / it\" for a thing: a masculine thing is on, a feminine thing is ona, a neuter thing is ono. Gdzie jest telefon? — On jest tu. (Where's the phone? — It's here.) Adjectives: the ending follows the gender: nowy dom, nowa kawa, nowe okno. See Nominative: naming things; adjective agreement . Learning new words: if the ending does not tell you, learn the noun with ten / ta / to: ta noc, ten tata. Examples Polish English Ten dom jest stary. This house is old. Ta kawa jest zimna. This coffee is cold. To okno jest małe. This window is small. Ten kolega jest bardzo miły. This friend is very nice. Gdzie jest lampa? — Ona jest tam. Where's the lamp? — It's over there. To muzeum jest nowe. This museum is new. Ta noc jest długa. This night is long. To dziecko jest zmęczone. This child is tired. Good to know ten, ta, to mean \"this\", not \"the\". Polish has no word for \"the\" or \"a\": To jest dom. (It's a house.) The to of to jest never changes: być (to be) and to jest / to są . In speech people often leave out the pronoun: Gdzie jest telefon? — Jest tu. Masculine nouns have smaller groups (people, animals, things). They start to matter with the accusative: Accusative: the direct object . Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Gdzie jest telefon? — Ono jest tu. → ✓ On jest tu. — things are on or ona by gender; ono is only for neuter nouns. ✗ ta tata → ✓ ten tata — men's words ending in -a are masculine. ✗ ten noc → ✓ ta noc — noc is feminine although it ends in a consonant. ✗ ta muzeum → ✓ to muzeum — nouns in -um are neuter. ✗ To jest ten dom. (for \"It's a house.\") → ✓ To jest dom. — ten means \"this\"; Polish has no articles. Practice Masculine, feminine or neuter? Write ten, ta or to. okno telefon książka kolega imię noc Choose on, ona or ono. Gdzie jest lampa? — jest tu. Gdzie jest dziecko? — jest w domu. (at home) Answers to ten ta ten to ta Ona Ono","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-gender-of-nouns"},{"id":"page:a1-cases-nominative","group":"pages","title":"Nominative: naming things; adjective agreement","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The dictionary form of a noun, used for the subject and after to jest. Adjectives agree: nowy dom, nowa praca, nowe auto.","text":"Polish nouns and adjectives change their endings to show their job in the sentence. These forms are called cases. The nominative (mianownik, questions: kto? co?) is the dictionary form: the form you name things with. Unlike English adjectives (one form, \"new\"), a Polish adjective agrees with the gender of its noun: nowy dom, nowa praca, nowe auto. How it works Singular. The plural comes at A2. Gender Adjective ending Example masculine -y (after k, g: -i) nowy dom, wysoki brat, drogi telefon feminine -a nowa praca, wysoka siostra, droga kawa neuter -e (after k, g: -ie) nowe auto, wysokie okno, drogie mieszkanie k and g cannot be followed by y in Polish, so they take i: wysoki, wysokie (never wysoky). Two questions ask for the nominative: Question About Example Kto to jest? people and animals To jest Marta. To jest kot. Co to jest? things To jest herbata. To jest nowy telefon. When to use it The subject of a sentence: Kawa jest gorąca. (The coffee is hot.) After to jest / to są: To jest mój nowy kolega. (This is my new friend.) An adjective after być: Dom jest stary. (The house is old.) Anna jest wysoka. (Anna is tall.) Naming and listing: dictionaries, menus and signs: kawa, herbata, sok. A noun alone after jestem / jest is different: Jestem studentem. See Instrumental after być and with z . Examples Polish English Co to jest? — To jest polska kawa. What's this? — It's Polish coffee. Kto to jest? — To jest pan Kowalczyk. Who's this? — It's Mr Kowalczyk. Mieszkanie jest małe, ale ładne. The flat is small but nice. Mój brat jest wysoki. My brother is tall. To jest bardzo dobra książka. This is a very good book. Gdzie jest nowy telefon? Where's the new phone? Ta herbata jest zimna! This tea is cold! To dziecko jest bardzo miłe. This child is very nice. Good to know The adjective usually comes before the noun: dobra kawa. In fixed names it comes after: język polski (the Polish language), kuchnia włoska (Italian cuisine). mój, moja, moje (my) agree the same way: mój dom, moja kawa, moje okno. A man's word ending in -a takes masculine adjectives: dobry tata, miły kolega. Adjectives of nationality take a small letter: polska kawa. Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ nowa dom → ✓ nowy dom — dom is masculine, so the adjective ends in -y. ✗ dobry kawa → ✓ dobra kawa — a feminine noun needs a feminine adjective. ✗ nowy auto → ✓ nowe auto — auto ends in -o, so it is neuter: -e. ✗ wysoky → ✓ wysoki — after k and g write i, not y. ✗ Kto to jest? (pointing at a lamp) → ✓ Co to jest? — kto is only for people and animals. Practice Put the adjective in the right form. (nowy) praca (stary) okno (wysoki) siostra (drogi) telefon (dobry) tata Kto or co? to jest? — To jest pani Nowak. to jest? — To jest lampa. Translate into Polish. The coffee is cold. Answers nowa stare wysoka drogi dobry Kto Co Kawa jest zimna.","level":"A1","category":"Cases","route":"#/a1/cases-nominative"},{"id":"page:a1-cases-instrumental-identity","group":"pages","title":"Instrumental after być and with z","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Jobs and nationalities after być take the instrumental: Jestem studentem. Ona jest lekarką. And z + instrumental: kawa z mlekiem.","text":"In English \"I am a student\" uses the same form of \"student\" as \"the student is here\". In Polish a job, nationality or role after a form of być changes its ending: Jestem studentem. Ona jest lekarką. This form is the instrumental (narzędnik, questions: kim? czym?). The same case follows z when it means \"with\": kawa z mlekiem (coffee with milk). Adjectives alone after być do not change: Jestem zmęczony. How it works Singular. The plural comes at A2. Gender Noun Adjective Examples masculine -em (after k, g: -iem) -ym (after k, g: -im) student → studentem, Polak → Polakiem, dobry lekarz → dobrym lekarzem feminine -a → -ą -a → -ą studentka → studentką, dobra lekarka → dobrą lekarką neuter -em (after k, g: -iem) -ym (after k, g: -im) mleko → mlekiem, małe dziecko → małym dzieckiem Men's words ending in -a take -ą like feminine nouns: tata → tatą, kolega → kolegą. When to use it Job, nationality or role after być: Jestem nauczycielką. (I'm a teacher. — a woman speaking) Marek jest Polakiem. (Marek is Polish.) With an adjective + noun: To jest dobry lekarz. but On jest dobrym lekarzem. (He's a good doctor.) z meaning \"with\": herbata z cytryną (tea with lemon), kanapka z serem (a cheese sandwich), Ewa z mężem (Ewa and her husband; mąż → mężem). Not after to jest: to jest + nominative. To jest student. (This is a student.) but On jest studentem. (He's a student.) Not for an adjective alone: Jestem zmęczona. (I'm tired. — a woman speaking) Examples Polish English Jestem studentem, a moja siostra jest lekarką. I'm a student, and my sister is a doctor. Kim jesteś z zawodu? — Jestem kelnerką. What do you do? — I'm a waitress. Pan Nowak jest Polakiem, a pani Smith jest Angielką. Mr Nowak is Polish, and Ms Smith is English. Mój tata jest bardzo dobrym kucharzem. My dad is a very good cook. Kawa z mlekiem, proszę. A coffee with milk, please. Herbata z cytryną czy z miodem? Tea with lemon or with honey? To jest Kasia z bratem. This is Kasia with her brother. On jest wysoki i bardzo miły. He's tall and very nice. Good to know ze instead of z before some clusters that start with s, z, ś: ze śmietaną (with cream), ze Zbyszkiem (with Zbyszek). z + a different case means \"from\": Jestem z Polski. That is the genitive: Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions . Nationality nouns take a capital letter: Jestem Kanadyjczykiem / Kanadyjką. Your name stays in the nominative: Jestem Tom. (never Tomem) Women may also use the masculine job title: Ona jest dobrym lekarzem. Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Jestem student. → ✓ Jestem studentem. — a job after być takes the instrumental. ✗ Ona jest lekarka. → ✓ Ona jest lekarką. — feminine nouns change -a to -ą. ✗ Jestem zmęczonym. → ✓ Jestem zmęczony. — an adjective alone stays in the nominative. ✗ kawa z mleko → ✓ kawa z mlekiem — z meaning \"with\" takes the instrumental. ✗ To jest studentem. → ✓ To jest student. — after to jest the noun does not change. Practice Put the word in brackets into the instrumental. Jestem (nauczyciel) . Anna jest (studentka) . Tomek jest (Polak) . Herbata z (cytryna) . Kawa z (mleko) . Instrumental or not? Choose. To jest (lekarz / lekarzem). Jestem (zmęczony / zmęczonym). Translate into Polish. She's a good doctor. Answers nauczycielem studentką Polakiem cytryną mlekiem lekarz zmęczony Ona jest dobrą lekarką. / Ona jest dobrym lekarzem.","level":"A1","category":"Cases","route":"#/a1/cases-instrumental-identity"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-personal-details","group":"pages","title":"Give and ask for personal details","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Say where you are from, where you live, how old you are and how to contact you. Skąd jesteś? Mam 30 lat. Mieszkam w Krakowie.","text":"These questions come up on every form and in every first conversation. Three work differently from English. Age uses mieć (\"have\"): Mam trzydzieści lat (\"I have thirty years\"), never \"I am thirty\". After z (from) and w (in) the name of a country or city changes its ending: Polska → z Polski, Kraków → w Krakowie. Learn these as ready phrases for now; the rules come with Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions and Locative: saying where you are . Key phrases Name Polish English Jak się pan nazywa? / Jak się pani nazywa? What's your name? (formal, to a man / to a woman) Nazywam się Jan Kowalski. My name is Jan Kowalski. Jak to się pisze? How do you spell that? Where from Polish English Skąd jesteś? Where are you from? (informal) Skąd pan jest? / Skąd pani jest? Where are you from? (formal) Jestem z Kanady / z Irlandii / ze Stanów. I'm from Canada / Ireland / the States. Where you live Polish English Gdzie mieszkasz? Where do you live? (informal) Gdzie pan mieszka? / Gdzie pani mieszka? Where do you live? (formal) Mieszkam w Krakowie / w Warszawie / w Londynie. I live in Kraków / Warsaw / London. Age Polish English Ile masz lat? How old are you? (informal) Ile pan ma lat? / Ile pani ma lat? How old are you? (formal) Mam dwadzieścia pięć lat. I'm twenty-five. Mam trzydzieści dwa lata. I'm thirty-two. Contact Polish English Jaki jest twój numer telefonu? What's your phone number? (informal) Jaki jest pana / pani numer telefonu? What's your phone number? (formal) Jaki jest pana / pani adres e-mail? What's your email address? (formal) Mój e-mail to ewa kropka nowak małpa poczta kropka pe el. My email is ewa dot nowak at poczta dot pl. Dialogue Sean signs up for a Polish course. Pani Agnieszka fills in the form. Pani Agnieszka: Dzień dobry. Jak się pan nazywa? (Hello. What's your name?) Sean: Nazywam się Sean Murphy. (My name is Sean Murphy.) Pani Agnieszka: Jak to się pisze? (How do you spell that?) Sean: S, E, A, N. (S, E, A, N.) Pani Agnieszka: Skąd pan jest? (Where are you from?) Sean: Jestem z Irlandii. (I'm from Ireland.) Pani Agnieszka: A gdzie pan mieszka? (And where do you live?) Sean: Mieszkam w Krakowie. (I live in Kraków.) Pani Agnieszka: Ile pan ma lat? (How old are you?) Sean: Mam dwadzieścia cztery lata. (I'm twenty-four.) Culture note Asking an adult Ile pan ma lat? is fine on a form, but in conversation it can sound too personal. In email addresses @ is małpa (\"monkey\") and the dot is kropka. Good to know lat or lata? After numbers ending in 2, 3, 4 (but not 12, 13, 14) say lata: dwadzieścia dwa lata. After all the others say lat: dwadzieścia pięć lat, dwanaście lat. One year old is rok. z becomes ze before some clusters: ze Stanów, ze Szkocji. Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Jestem trzydzieści lat. → ✓ Mam trzydzieści lat. — age uses mieć (\"have\"), not być. ✗ Mam dwadzieścia dwa lat. → ✓ Mam dwadzieścia dwa lata. — after a number ending in 2, 3, 4 say lata. ✗ Jestem z Kanada. → ✓ Jestem z Kanady. — after z the country changes its ending. ✗ Mieszkam w Kraków. → ✓ Mieszkam w Krakowie. — after w the city changes its ending. ✗ Skąd jesteś? (to a stranger) → ✓ Skąd pan jest? / Skąd pani jest? — use pan / pani with people you don't know. Practice Complete the answers. Skąd jesteś? — Jestem Kanady. Gdzie mieszkasz? — Mieszkam Warszawie. Ile masz lat? — dwadzieścia lat. lat or lata? Mam trzydzieści trzy . Mam czterdzieści . Translate into Polish. Where are you from? (formal, to a woman) Where do you live? (informal) How do you spell that? Answers z w Mam lata lat Skąd pani jest? Gdzie mieszkasz? Jak to się pisze?","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-personal-details"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-family-people","group":"pages","title":"Family, describing people, nationalities, jobs","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Words for your family, simple adjectives for people, and male and female forms of nationalities and jobs. Mój brat jest wysoki.","text":"English has one word for most jobs and nationalities: \"a doctor\", \"an American\". Polish usually has a male and a female form: lekarz / lekarka, Amerykanin / Amerykanka. Adjectives that describe people change with gender too: wysoki brat, wysoka siostra. Nationality nouns take a capital letter (Polak), but nationality adjectives do not (polski). Words Family Polish English rodzina (f) family mama (f) / matka (f) mum / mother tata (m) / ojciec (m) dad / father rodzice (plural) parents brat (m) brother siostra (f) sister syn (m) son córka (f) daughter mąż (m) husband żona (f) wife dziecko (n), plural dzieci child, children babcia (f) grandma dziadek (m) grandpa ciocia (f) aunt wujek (m) uncle chłopak (m) boyfriend dziewczyna (f) girlfriend Describing people (masculine / feminine) Polish English wysoki / wysoka tall niski / niska short młody / młoda young stary / stara old ładny / ładna pretty, nice-looking przystojny handsome (about men) miły / miła nice, kind sympatyczny / sympatyczna likeable, friendly zabawny / zabawna funny Nationalities (man / woman) Polish English Polak / Polka Pole (Polish) Anglik / Angielka Englishman / Englishwoman Amerykanin / Amerykanka American Kanadyjczyk / Kanadyjka Canadian Irlandczyk / Irlandka Irishman / Irishwoman Jobs (man / woman) Polish English lekarz / lekarka doctor nauczyciel / nauczycielka teacher student / studentka student kelner / kelnerka waiter / waitress prawnik / prawniczka lawyer informatyk / informatyczka IT specialist Word partners Polish English Mój brat jest wysoki. My brother is tall. Moja siostra jest lekarką. My sister is a doctor. Jestem Amerykaninem. / Jestem Amerykanką. I'm American. (a man / a woman) To jest moja żona, Ola. This is my wife, Ola. mąż i żona husband and wife starszy brat older brother młodsza siostra younger sister Babcia jest bardzo miła. Grandma is very kind. polska rodzina a Polish family Mój tata jest nauczycielem. My dad is a teacher. Good to know mama and tata are what people say; matka and ojciec sound more formal. sympatyczny means \"likeable, friendly\", not \"sympathetic\". dziewczyna is also any \"girl\", and chłopak any \"boy\" or young man. Jobs and nationalities after być take the instrumental: Jestem Polką. See Instrumental after być and with z . Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ moja brat → ✓ mój brat — brat is masculine, so \"my\" is mój. ✗ Jestem polski. → ✓ Jestem Polakiem. / Jestem Polką. — to say your nationality use the noun, with a capital letter. ✗ To jest Polski film. → ✓ To jest polski film. — nationality adjectives take a small letter. ✗ Ona jest lekarz. → ✓ Ona jest lekarką. — the job needs the instrumental: lekarką (lekarzem is also used for women). ✗ dziecki (for \"children\") → ✓ dzieci — the plural of dziecko is irregular: dzieci. Practice Give the female form. lekarz → nauczyciel → Kanadyjczyk → Complete the family. mąż i brat i syn i Translate into Polish. My brother is tall. My mum is a teacher. Answers lekarka nauczycielka Kanadyjka żona siostra córka Mój brat jest wysoki. Moja mama jest nauczycielką.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-family-people"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-present-conjugations","group":"pages","title":"Present tense: the three conjugation patterns","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Most Polish verbs follow one of three patterns in the present: czytam, czytasz; mówię, mówisz; piszę, piszesz; pracuję.","text":"English verbs hardly change in the present: \"I read, you read, she reads\". A Polish verb has six endings, one for each person, so the ending alone tells you who. Polish also has only one present tense: czytam means \"I read\", \"I'm reading\" and \"I do read\". Most verbs follow one of three patterns. How it works Pattern 1: -am, -asz Person czytać (read) mieszkać (live) ja czytam mieszkam ty czytasz mieszkasz on, ona, ono, pan, pani czyta mieszka my czytamy mieszkamy wy czytacie mieszkacie oni, one, państwo czytają mieszkają Like this: znać (know a person or place), pytać (ask), słuchać (listen), oglądać (watch). Pattern 2: -ę, -isz / -ysz Person mówić (speak) tańczyć (dance) ja mówię tańczę ty mówisz tańczysz on, ona, ono, pan, pani mówi tańczy my mówimy tańczymy wy mówicie tańczycie oni, one, państwo mówią tańczą Like this: robić (do, make), lubić (like). After cz, sz, rz, ż write y: tańczysz. Pattern 3: -ę, -esz Person pisać (write) pracować (work) ja piszę pracuję ty piszesz pracujesz on, ona, ono, pan, pani pisze pracuje my piszemy pracujemy wy piszecie pracujecie oni, one, państwo piszą pracują All verbs in -ować go like pracować: kupować → kupuję (buy), gotować → gotuję (cook), studiować → studiuję (study at university). Pisać changes s to sz in every form. Pić (drink) also goes here: piję, pijesz, pije, pijemy, pijecie, piją. The infinitive does not always show the pattern (czytać → czytam, but pisać → piszę), so learn every new verb with two forms: ja and ty. When to use it Now: Co robisz? — Czytam. (What are you doing? — I'm reading.) Habits and routines: Rano piję sok. (In the morning I drink juice.) Facts about you: Mieszkam w Krakowie i pracuję w banku. (I live in Kraków and I work in a bank.) Questions need no \"do\": Mówisz po angielsku? (Do you speak English?) Examples Polish English Gdzie mieszkasz? — Mieszkam w Gdańsku. Where do you live? — I live in Gdańsk. Mówię po polsku, ale bardzo wolno. I speak Polish, but very slowly. Pan Tadeusz pracuje w szkole. Mr Tadeusz works at a school. Wieczorem oglądamy film. In the evening we watch a film. Co robicie? — Gotujemy obiad. What are you doing? — We're cooking lunch. Kasia pisze list. Kasia is writing a letter. Moi rodzice nie mówią po angielsku. My parents don't speak English. Czy państwo tańczą? Do you dance? (formal, to a couple) Good to know rozumieć (understand) has its own small pattern: rozumiem, rozumiesz, rozumie, rozumiemy, rozumiecie, rozumieją. Nie rozumiem is a phrase you will need every day. Place phrases such as w banku, w szkole, w biurze (in an office) are ready chunks for now; the rule comes with Locative: saying where you are . A few very common verbs are irregular (mam, idę, jem, chcę): mieć and other common irregular verbs . Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Jestem czytam. → ✓ Czytam. — \"I am reading\" is one word; never add być. ✗ Mówię polski. → ✓ Mówię po polsku. — you speak a language \"the Polish way\": po polsku. ✗ Pracowam w szkole. → ✓ Pracuję w szkole. — verbs in -ować change to -uję. ✗ Oni czyta. → ✓ Oni czytają. — each person has its own ending. ✗ Ja mieszkam w Krakowie, ja pracuję w biurze. → ✓ Mieszkam w Krakowie i pracuję w biurze. — repeating ja sounds unnatural; the ending already says \"I\". Practice Put the verb in the right form. Ja (czytać) list. Ty (mówić) po polsku? Ona (pracować) w banku. My (mieszkać) w Warszawie. Oni (pisać) e-mail. Wy (lubić) jazz? Correct the mistake. Jestem oglądam film. Translate into Polish. Where do you work? (to a friend) Answers czytam mówisz pracuje mieszkamy piszą lubicie Oglądam film. Gdzie pracujesz?","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: aspect and tenses","route":"#/a1/verbs-present-conjugations"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-common-irregular","group":"pages","title":"mieć and other common irregular verbs","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The present forms of seven verbs you need every day: mam, jem, wiem, idę, jadę, chcę, mogę.","text":"In English the most common verbs are the irregular ones (\"be, have, go\"), and Polish is the same. The seven verbs on this page do not follow the patterns of Present tense: the three conjugation patterns , but most endings are the ones you know (-sz, -my, -cie); what changes is the stem. Two differences from English: Polish has two verbs for \"go\" (iść on foot, jechać by car, bus or train) and two for \"know\" (wiedzieć a fact, znać a person or place). How it works Verbs with -m for ja: Person mieć (have) jeść (eat) wiedzieć (know a fact) ja mam jem wiem ty masz jesz wiesz on, ona, ono, pan, pani ma je wie my mamy jemy wiemy wy macie jecie wiecie oni, one, państwo mają jedzą wiedzą Going: Person iść (go on foot) jechać (go by vehicle) ja idę jadę ty idziesz jedziesz on, ona, ono, pan, pani idzie jedzie my idziemy jedziemy wy idziecie jedziecie oni, one, państwo idą jadą Wanting and being able: Person chcieć (want) móc (can, be able) ja chcę mogę ty chcesz możesz on, ona, ono, pan, pani chce może my chcemy możemy wy chcecie możecie oni, one, państwo chcą mogą In bold: the ja and oni forms have a different stem from the rest: idę – idziesz, jadę – jedziesz, mogę – możesz. When to use it mieć: having, and age. Mamy nowe mieszkanie. (We have a new flat.) Mam dwadzieścia lat. (I'm twenty.) jeść: eating. Co jesz? — Jem obiad. (What are you eating? — I'm having lunch.) wiedzieć: knowing a fact, often before gdzie, kto, co: Nie wiem, gdzie on jest. (I don't know where he is.) For people and places use znać: Znasz Kraków? (Do you know Kraków?) iść and jechać: going now. Idę do domu. (I'm going home, on foot.) Jadę do Krakowa. (I'm going to Kraków, by car, bus or train.) More in Going places: iść and jechać . chcieć and móc: + a noun or a verb. Chcę piwo. (I want a beer.) Czy mogę? (May I?) With a second verb in the infinitive: lubić, chcieć, móc, musieć, umieć + infinitive . Examples Polish English Masz czas? — Nie wiem. Have you got time? — I don't know. Mają duży dom i mały ogród. They have a big house and a small garden. Co jecie? — Jemy śniadanie. What are you eating? — We're having breakfast. Dzieci jedzą obiad w szkole. The children eat lunch at school. Dokąd idziesz? — Idę do pracy. Where are you going? — I'm going to work. Jutro pani Ewa jedzie do Gdańska. Tomorrow Mrs Ewa is going to Gdańsk. Wiecie, kto to jest? Do you know who that is? Mogę tu usiąść? — Tak, proszę. Can I sit here? — Yes, please do. Good to know jedzą (they eat) and jadą (they are going) look alike. Listen for jedz- against jad-. do domu, do pracy, do Krakowa (home, to work, to Kraków) are ready phrases for now; the rule comes with Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions . In everyday speech Gdzie idziesz? is common for Dokąd idziesz? Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Wiem Kraków. → ✓ Znam Kraków. — wiedzieć is for facts; people and places take znać. ✗ Idę do Krakowa samochodem. → ✓ Jadę do Krakowa samochodem. — iść is only on foot. ✗ Oni jadą obiad. → ✓ Oni jedzą obiad. — jedzą is \"they eat\"; jadą is \"they are going\". ✗ Ty jadziesz. → ✓ Ty jedziesz. — the stem changes: jadę, but jedziesz, jedzie. ✗ Chcę jem. → ✓ Chcę jeść. — after chcę the second verb is the infinitive. Practice Put the verb in the right form. Ja (mieć) czas. Oni (jeść) śniadanie. Ty (wiedzieć) , gdzie jest Ola? My (iść) do domu. Ona (jechać) do Krakowa. Wy (chcieć) piwo? Dzisiaj nie (móc) . (ja) Translate into Polish. I don't know. Answers mam jedzą wiesz idziemy jedzie chcecie mogę Nie wiem.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: aspect and tenses","route":"#/a1/verbs-common-irregular"},{"id":"page:a1-cases-accusative","group":"pages","title":"Accusative: the direct object","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The form for the thing or person an action goes to. Mam brata. Lubię kawę.","text":"English shows who does what by word order: \"Anna loves Tom\" is not \"Tom loves Anna\". Polish shows it with endings. The person or thing the action goes to (the direct object) takes the accusative (biernik, questions: kogo? co?). Mam brata. Lubię kawę. The good news: for many nouns the accusative is the same as the dictionary form. How it works Singular. The plural comes at A2: Accusative plural and masculine personal accusative . Gender Noun Adjective Nominative → accusative masculine: people and animals -a -ego dobry brat → Mam dobrego brata. masculine: things no change no change nowy telefon → Mam nowy telefon. feminine -a → -ę -a → -ą dobra kawa → Piję dobrą kawę. neuter no change no change małe piwo → Poproszę małe piwo. So only two groups really change: masculine nouns for people and animals, and feminine nouns in -a. Feminine nouns without -a do not change: Poproszę sól. When to use it After most verbs that take an object: mieć, lubić, znać, czytać, pić, jeść, kupować, oglądać, kochać. Czytam książkę. (I'm reading a book.) When you order or ask for something: Poproszę kawę i wodę. (A coffee and a water, please.) With days of the week after w: w środę (on Wednesday), w sobotę (on Saturday). Not after nie. A negated object takes the genitive: Mam czas. → Nie mam czasu. See Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions . Examples Polish English Mam brata i siostrę. I have a brother and a sister. Lubię polską kuchnię. I like Polish food. Czytasz książkę czy gazetę? Are you reading a book or a newspaper? Poproszę herbatę z cytryną. A tea with lemon, please. Anna ma małego psa. Anna has a small dog. Kupuję nowy telefon. I'm buying a new phone. Jem kanapkę i piję sok. I'm eating a sandwich and drinking juice. Znasz mojego kolegę? Do you know my friend? Good to know Masculine nouns that end in -a (kolega, tata, mężczyzna) take -ę like feminine nouns, but their adjectives stay masculine: Znam twojego tatę. pani becomes panią: Przepraszam panią. (Excuse me, ma'am.) Some nouns lose an e before the ending: pies → psa, ojciec → ojca. In everyday speech a few masculine things take -a like animals: Jem banana. Piszę maila. Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Lubię kawa. → ✓ Lubię kawę. — the object needs its ending; word order does not mark it in Polish. ✗ Mam brat. → ✓ Mam brata. — masculine people and animals take -a. ✗ Piję dobra kawę. → ✓ Piję dobrą kawę. — the adjective changes too: -a → -ą. ✗ Kupuję nowego samochoda. → ✓ Kupuję nowy samochód. — masculine things do not change. ✗ To jest kawę. → ✓ To jest kawa. — after to jest use the nominative. Practice Put the words in brackets into the accusative. Mam (siostra) . Lubisz (herbata) ? Poproszę (zupa pomidorowa) . Tomek ma (kot) . Czytam (ciekawa książka) . Mamy (nowy nauczyciel) . Kupuję (mleko) i (chleb) . Translate into Polish. I have a brother. Answers siostrę herbatę zupę pomidorową kota ciekawą książkę nowego nauczyciela mleko, chleb Mam brata.","level":"A1","category":"Cases","route":"#/a1/cases-accusative"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-questions-negation","group":"pages","title":"Questions and negation","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Ask yes / no questions with czy, use the question words co, kto, gdzie, kiedy, jak, ile, dlaczego, and say no with nie.","text":"English needs a helper verb for questions and negatives: \"Do you work?\", \"I don't work\". Polish has no helper. A question is the normal sentence with czy in front (or just a rising voice): Czy pracujesz? A negative is nie in front of the verb: Nie pracuję. One more difference: words like \"nobody\", \"nothing\" and \"never\" still need nie with the verb, so Polish says \"nobody not knows\": Nikt nie wie. How it works Yes / no questions: Statement Question Answer Mówisz po polsku. Czy mówisz po polsku? Tak, mówię. / Nie, nie mówię. Pan jest z Polski. Czy pan jest z Polski? Tak. / Nie, jestem z Irlandii. Question words: Polish English Example co what Co robisz? kto who Kto to jest? gdzie where Gdzie mieszkasz? skąd where from Skąd jesteś? kiedy when Kiedy masz czas? jak how Jak się masz? ile how much, how many Ile to kosztuje? dlaczego why Dlaczego nie jesz? — Bo nie jestem głodny. jaki, jaka, jakie what … like, what kind of Jaka jest twoja szkoła? The question word comes first; the rest keeps normal order: Gdzie pracuje Ola? or Gdzie Ola pracuje? Jaki agrees with its noun: jaki dom, jaka kawa. Negation: Positive Negative Rozumiem. Nie rozumiem. To jest mój telefon. To nie jest mój telefon. Ktoś wie. (Somebody knows.) Nikt nie wie. (Nobody knows.) Rozumiem wszystko. (I understand everything.) Nic nie rozumiem. (I don't understand anything.) Pracuję w niedzielę. Nigdy nie pracuję w niedzielę. (I never work on Sundays.) When to use it Yes / no questions with czy: Czy masz czas? (Have you got time?) Yes / no questions without czy: in speech, just raise your voice: Masz czas? Questions with a question word, which comes first: Gdzie pracuje Ola? (Where does Ola work?) Do not add czy. Saying no: nie goes directly before the verb: Nie mieszkam tu. (I don't live here.) Answering \"no\" often gives two nie: Nie, nie mieszkam. \"Nobody, nothing, never\": nikt, nic, nigdy + nie + verb: Nigdy nie mam czasu. (I never have time.) Examples Polish English Czy pani mówi po angielsku? Do you speak English? (to a woman) Gdzie jest dworzec? Where is the station? Kiedy jedziesz do Gdańska? — W piątek. When are you going to Gdańsk? — On Friday. Dlaczego Marta nie je? — Bo jest zmęczona. Why isn't Marta eating? — Because she's tired. Przepraszam, nie rozumiem. Sorry, I don't understand. Kuba nie jest lekarzem, jest nauczycielem. Kuba isn't a doctor, he's a teacher. Nikt nie wie, gdzie jest klucz. Nobody knows where the key is. Jak masz na imię? — Paweł. What's your name? — Paweł. Good to know Kto and co take the \"he / she\" form of the verb: Kto ma czas? (Who has time?) Co jest w lodówce? (What's in the fridge?) Dlaczego? is answered with bo (because). After nie an object changes its ending (Mam czas. → Nie mam czasu.): see Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions . Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Rozumiem nie. → ✓ Nie rozumiem. — nie goes before the verb, not after it. ✗ Nie, rozumiem. (for \"No, I don't understand\") → ✓ Nie, nie rozumiem. — the first nie is \"no\", the second makes the verb negative. ✗ Nikt wie. → ✓ Nikt nie wie. — nikt, nic, nigdy need nie with the verb. ✗ Gdzie jesteś z? → ✓ Skąd jesteś? — \"where … from\" is one word: skąd. ✗ Co jest twoje imię? → ✓ Jak masz na imię? — Polish asks \"how\" about your name. Practice Make the sentence negative. Rozumiem. Pracuję w sobotę. To jest moja kawa. Choose the question word: gdzie, kiedy, skąd, dlaczego. mieszkasz? — W Poznaniu. jesteś? — Z Kanady. masz czas? — Jutro. Correct the mistake. Nikt wie. Translate into Polish. Do you speak Polish? (to a friend) Answers Nie rozumiem. Nie pracuję w sobotę. To nie jest moja kawa. Gdzie Skąd Kiedy Nikt nie wie. Czy mówisz po polsku? / Mówisz po polsku?","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-questions-negation"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-food-drink","group":"pages","title":"Food and drink","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Everyday food and drink, the three meals of the day, and phrases for eating and drinking. Herbata z cytryną. Smacznego!","text":"Food words are easy to learn but have two traps for English speakers. First, after jeść, pić, lubić and poproszę the food is the object, so feminine words change: kawa → Piję kawę. Second, Polish meals do not match English ones one to one: obiad is the main hot meal of the day, often eaten in the afternoon, so it is \"lunch\" or \"dinner\" depending on the time. Words Meals Polish English śniadanie (n) breakfast obiad (m) lunch, the main meal kolacja (f) supper, evening meal deser (m) dessert Drinks Polish English woda (f) water kawa (f) coffee herbata (f) tea mleko (n) milk sok (m) juice piwo (n) beer wino (n) wine Bread, breakfast and basics Polish English chleb (m) bread bułka (f) bread roll masło (n) butter ser (m) cheese szynka (f) ham jajko (n) egg kanapka (f) sandwich miód (m) honey cukier (m) sugar sól (f) salt Main meals Polish English zupa (f) soup mięso (n) meat kurczak (m) chicken ryba (f) fish kiełbasa (f) sausage ziemniaki (plural) potatoes ryż (m) rice makaron (m) pasta, noodles pierogi (plural) dumplings sałatka (f) salad Fruit and vegetables Polish English owoce (plural) fruit warzywa (plural) vegetables jabłko (n) apple banan (m) banana pomarańcza (f) orange truskawki (plural) strawberries pomidor (m) tomato ogórek (m) cucumber cebula (f) onion marchewka (f) carrot Sweet things Polish English ciasto (n) cake czekolada (f) chocolate lody (plural) ice cream Word partners Polish English herbata z cytryną tea with lemon kawa z mlekiem coffee with milk kanapka z serem a cheese sandwich zupa pomidorowa tomato soup sok pomarańczowy orange juice woda gazowana / niegazowana sparkling / still water Jem śniadanie o siódmej. I have breakfast at seven. Na obiad jemy rybę i ziemniaki. We're having fish and potatoes for lunch. Piję kawę bez cukru. I drink coffee without sugar. Jestem głodny. / Jestem głodna. I'm hungry. (a man / a woman) Ta zupa jest bardzo smaczna. This soup is very tasty. Smacznego! Enjoy your meal! Good to know Smacznego! is what you say before people start eating. The answer is Dziękuję, nawzajem. (Thank you, you too.) lody, ziemniaki, pierogi and truskawki are plural, so they take są: Lody są zimne. makaron is any pasta, not only macaroni: makaron z sosem pomidorowym (pasta with tomato sauce). o siódmej (at seven) and bez cukru (without sugar) are ready phrases; the rules come with Tell the time and say when things happen and Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions . Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Lubię kawa. → ✓ Lubię kawę. — after lubić a feminine noun takes -ę (accusative). ✗ Poproszę jedna kawa. → ✓ Poproszę kawę. — Polish has no word for \"a\"; jedna means \"one\" (Poproszę jedną kawę is fine if you mean one). ✗ Lody jest dobry. → ✓ Lody są dobre. — lody is plural in Polish. ✗ kanapka z ser → ✓ kanapka z serem — z meaning \"with\" takes the instrumental. ✗ Jem sałatę z pomidorem i serem. (for a mixed salad) → ✓ Jem sałatkę z pomidorem i serem. — sałatka is a salad; sałata is lettuce. Practice Write the gender: (m), (f) or (n). ser zupa jabłko sok Complete the phrase. herbata z (lemon) kawa z (milk) Translate into Polish. I'm hungry. (a woman speaking) I like tea. Answers (m) (f) (n) (m) cytryną mlekiem Jestem głodna. Lubię herbatę.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-food-drink"},{"id":"page:a1-cases-genitive-basics","group":"pages","title":"Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The case after nie, for \"of\" and after do, z, od, bez, dla. Nie mam czasu. To jest dom brata.","text":"English says \"I have time\" and \"I don't have time\" with the same word. In Polish the object changes when you add nie: Mam czas. → Nie mam czasu. This form is the genitive (dopełniacz, questions: kogo? czego?). It also does the job of English \"of\" and \"'s\" (dom brata, \"my brother's house\"), and it follows five very common prepositions: do, z (from), od, bez, dla. How it works Singular. The plural comes at A2. Gender Noun Adjective Nominative → genitive masculine -a or -u -ego dobry brat → dobrego brata, czas → czasu feminine -y (after k, g, l, j and in -ia: -i) -ej zimna kawa → zimnej kawy, książka → książki neuter -a -ego zimne piwo → zimnego piwa Masculine: people and animals almost always take -a (brata, psa, kota; for tata, kolega see below). Things take -a or -u, so learn the genitive with the word: -a -u chleb → chleba czas → czasu ser → sera dom → domu Kraków → Krakowa sklep → sklepu Gdańsk → Gdańska samochód → samochodu Feminine -i: Polska → Polski, Ola → Oli, kuchnia → kuchni. When to use it The object after nie: Nie mam brata. (I don't have a brother.) Nie lubię kawy. (I don't like coffee.) nie ma = \"there isn't\" or \"is not here\": Nie ma mleka. (There's no milk.) Ani nie ma. (Ania isn't here.) \"of\" and \"'s\": the owner comes after the thing: dom brata (my brother's house), imię psa (the dog's name). After do (to), z (from), od (from a person, since), bez (without), dla (for): Jadę do Krakowa. Jestem z Polski. To list od mamy. Kawa bez cukru. To dla Oli. Only the object of nie changes. A noun after nie jest stays as it was: To nie jest mój telefon. Kuba nie jest lekarzem. Examples Polish English Przepraszam, nie mam czasu. Sorry, I don't have time. W domu nie ma chleba. There's no bread at home. To jest samochód taty. This is Dad's car. Idę do sklepu. I'm going to the shop. Paweł jest z Wrocławia. Paweł is from Wrocław. Mam prezent od siostry. I've got a present from my sister. Herbata bez cukru, proszę. Tea without sugar, please. Ta kawa jest dla pani Ewy. This coffee is for Mrs Ewa. Good to know z + genitive is \"from\": z Polski. z + instrumental is \"with\": z mlekiem. Same word, different ending: Instrumental after być and with z . Watch the mobile e: cukier → cukru, pies → psa, ojciec → ojca. Men's words in -a take -y like feminine nouns: kolega → kolegi, tata → taty. u + genitive means \"at someone's place\": u babci (at Grandma's). dużo (a lot of) and mało (little) take the genitive too: dużo czasu. Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Nie mam czas. → ✓ Nie mam czasu. — after nie the object takes the genitive. ✗ Nie lubię kawę. → ✓ Nie lubię kawy. — the accusative kawę becomes the genitive kawy after nie. ✗ Nie ma mleko. → ✓ Nie ma mleka. — nie ma (\"there isn't\") always takes the genitive. ✗ brata dom → ✓ dom brata — the owner comes after the thing, not before it as in English. ✗ kawa bez cukier → ✓ kawa bez cukru — bez takes the genitive. Practice Put the word in brackets into the genitive. Nie mam (czas) . Nie ma (woda) . Jadę do (Gdańsk) . (-a) Kawa bez (mleko) . To jest pokój (siostra) . Prezent dla (mama) . Correct the mistake. Nie lubię herbatę. Translate into Polish. I don't have a car. Answers czasu wody Gdańska mleka siostry mamy Nie lubię herbaty. Nie mam samochodu.","level":"A1","category":"Cases","route":"#/a1/cases-genitive-basics"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-modal-verbs-basics","group":"pages","title":"lubić, chcieć, móc, musieć, umieć + infinitive","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Say what you like, want, can, must and know how to do: a form of lubić, chcieć, móc, musieć or umieć + an infinitive.","text":"Like English \"I can swim\" or \"I want to sleep\", Polish puts a second verb after these five verbs, and the second verb is the infinitive (the dictionary form): Chcę spać. There is no \"to\". The differences: English \"can\" is two Polish verbs (móc \"be able, be allowed\", umieć \"know how\"), and nie muszę means \"I don't have to\", not \"I mustn't\". How it works The first verb changes, the infinitive does not: Person musieć (must, have to) umieć (know how) ja muszę umiem ty musisz umiesz on, ona, ono, pan, pani musi umie my musimy umiemy wy musicie umiecie oni, one, państwo muszą umieją The other three you already know: lubię, lubisz… Present tense: the three conjugation patterns ; chcę, chcesz… and mogę, możesz… mieć and other common irregular verbs . Verb + infinitive: Verb + infinitive English lubię czytać I like reading. chcę spać I want to sleep. mogę przyjść I can come. muszę iść I must go. / I have to go. umiem pływać I can swim. (I know how) Most infinitives end in -ć (czytać, mówić); a few end in -c (móc) or -ść (iść, jeść). When to use it lubić: things you like doing. Lubię gotować. (I like cooking.) chcieć: wishes and plans. Chcemy jechać do Zakopanego. (We want to go to Zakopane.) móc: possible or allowed, and polite requests. Czy mogę zapłacić kartą? (Can I pay by card?) musieć: must, have to. Muszę pracować w sobotę. (I have to work on Saturday.) Nie musisz czekać. (You don't have to wait.) umieć: skills you have learnt. Umiesz pływać? (Can you swim?) Examples Polish English Chcesz iść do kina? Do you want to go to the cinema? Nie mogę dzisiaj, muszę pracować. I can't today, I have to work. Ola nie umie gotować, ale lubi jeść. Ola can't cook, but she likes eating. Moi rodzice lubią tańczyć. My parents like dancing. Czy mogę tu usiąść? May I sit here? Musimy kupić chleb. We have to buy bread. Pan Marek umie mówić po niemiecku. Mr Marek can speak German. Nie musicie jutro przychodzić. You don't have to come tomorrow. Good to know lubić also takes a noun (Lubię kawę), and after nie the noun takes the genitive: Nie lubię kawy. See Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions . Czy mogę …? is the polite way to ask for permission: Czy mogę zapytać? (May I ask?) For \"you mustn't\" Polish uses nie wolno: Tu nie wolno palić. (You mustn't smoke here.) Learn it as a phrase for now. Mówię po polsku and Umiem mówić po polsku are both fine for \"I speak Polish\". Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Lubię czytam. → ✓ Lubię czytać. — the second verb is the infinitive. ✗ Znam pływać. → ✓ Umiem pływać. — skills take umieć; znać is for people and places. ✗ Nie musisz palić tutaj. (for \"You mustn't smoke here\") → ✓ Tu nie wolno palić. — nie musisz means \"you don't have to\". ✗ Ja musę iść. → ✓ Muszę iść. — s changes to sz in muszę and muszą. ✗ Czy mogę mieć kawę? → ✓ Poproszę kawę. — \"Can I have…?\" is not translated word for word. Practice Put the first verb in the right form. Ja (musieć) iść. Ty (umieć) pływać? Oni (chcieć) spać. My (móc) przyjść jutro. Ona (lubić) gotować. Correct the mistake. Chcę jem. móc or umieć? pływać, ale dzisiaj nie mogę, bo jestem chory. (ja) Translate into Polish. I have to work. Answers muszę umiesz chcą możemy lubi Chcę jeść. Umiem Muszę pracować.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: aspect and tenses","route":"#/a1/verbs-modal-verbs-basics"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-ordering-shopping","group":"pages","title":"Order in a cafe and buy things","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Order food and drink, ask what things cost, and pay. Poproszę kawę. Ile to kosztuje? Czy mogę prosić o rachunek?","text":"In English you order with a question: \"Can I have a coffee?\" Polish uses one short word, poproszę (\"I'll ask for\"), and the thing you want is the object, so it takes the accusative: Poproszę kawę. Saying Chcę (\"I want\") to a waiter sounds blunt. Prices work like age: the word for the money changes after the number (dwa złote, pięć złotych). Key phrases Ordering Polish English Poproszę kawę z mlekiem. A coffee with milk, please. Dla mnie herbata. Tea for me. Czy jest sok pomarańczowy? Do you have orange juice? Co pan poleca? / Co pani poleca? What do you recommend? (formal, to a man / to a woman) Na wynos, proszę. To take away, please. To wszystko, dziękuję. That's all, thank you. What staff say Polish English Co podać? What can I get you? Na miejscu czy na wynos? For here or to take away? Coś jeszcze? Anything else? Niestety nie ma. Sorry, we don't have any. Proszę bardzo. Here you are. Prices and paying Polish English Ile to kosztuje? How much is it? Ile kosztuje ten ser? How much is this cheese? Ile płacę? How much do I owe? Czy mogę prosić o rachunek? Could I have the bill, please? Czy mogę zapłacić kartą? Can I pay by card? Gotówką. In cash. Dialogue Marta is in a cafe. The waiter, Kamil, speaks to her formally. Kamil: Dzień dobry. Co dla pani? (Hello. What can I get you?) Marta: Poproszę kawę z mlekiem i sernik. (A coffee with milk and a cheesecake, please.) Kamil: Na miejscu czy na wynos? (For here or to take away?) Marta: Na miejscu. Czy jest woda niegazowana? (For here. Do you have still water?) Kamil: Niestety nie ma. Jest tylko gazowana. (Sorry, we don't. There's only sparkling.) Marta: To nie, dziękuję. Ile płacę? (Then no, thank you. How much do I owe?) Kamil: Dwadzieścia osiem złotych. (Twenty-eight złoty.) Marta: Czy mogę zapłacić kartą? (Can I pay by card?) Kamil: Oczywiście. Proszę bardzo. (Of course. Here you are.) Culture note In a restaurant you usually ask for the bill: the waiter does not bring it without being asked. A tip of about ten percent is common in restaurants. Reszty nie trzeba means \"Keep the change\". Good to know złoty after numbers works like rok, lata, lat: jeden złoty, dwa / trzy / cztery złote, pięć złotych (and dwadzieścia dwa złote, dwanaście złotych). One hundredth of a złoty is a grosz: pięćdziesiąt groszy. Poproszę takes the accusative: poproszę wodę, poproszę sok. See Accusative: the direct object . Nie ma + genitive: Nie ma mleka. (There's no milk.) See Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions . kartą and gotówką are instrumental: \"by card\", \"in cash\". Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Chcę kawę. (to a waiter) → ✓ Poproszę kawę. — chcę sounds blunt; poproszę is the polite way to order. ✗ Poproszę kawa. → ✓ Poproszę kawę. — the thing you order takes the accusative. ✗ Czy mogę zapłacić z kartą? → ✓ Czy mogę zapłacić kartą? — \"by card\" is the instrumental alone, with no z. ✗ dwadzieścia pięć złote → ✓ dwadzieścia pięć złotych — from 5 up say złotych, except after 22–24, 32–34 and so on (numbers ending in 2, 3, 4, but not 12–14). Practice Complete the phrases. kawę z mlekiem. (A coffee with milk, please.) Ile to ? (How much is it?) Czy mogę zapłacić ? (by card) Czy mogę prosić o ? (the bill) złote or złotych? trzy dziesięć Translate into Polish. For here or to take away? That's all, thank you. Answers Poproszę kosztuje kartą rachunek złote złotych Na miejscu czy na wynos? To wszystko, dziękuję.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-ordering-shopping"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-time-dates","group":"pages","title":"Tell the time and say when things happen","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Good to know","Culture note","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Ask and tell the time, say at what time things happen, and use rano, wieczorem, dzisiaj, jutro. Jest trzecia. O ósmej.","text":"English tells the time with ordinary numbers: \"It's eight\", \"at eight\". Polish uses the \"first, second, third\" words in the feminine, because godzina (hour) is feminine: Jest ósma. (It's eight, literally \"the eighth\".) After o (at) the ending changes: o ósmej. Learn the two sets of forms below as phrases. Watch out for \"half\": wpół do dziewiątej is 8:30, not 9:30. Key phrases Asking and telling the time Polish English Która (jest) godzina? What time is it? Przepraszam, która godzina? Excuse me, what time is it? Jest pierwsza. It's one o'clock. Jest ósma trzydzieści. It's eight thirty. Jest wpół do dziewiątej. It's half past eight. Saying when Polish English O której? At what time? O ósmej. At eight. O siódmej wieczorem. At seven in the evening. Pracuję od dziewiątej do piątej. I work from nine to five. Kiedy? — W sobotę. When? — On Saturday. Parts of the day and days Polish English rano in the morning po południu in the afternoon wieczorem in the evening w nocy at night dzisiaj / dziś today jutro tomorrow w sobotę wieczorem on Saturday evening w weekend at the weekend Dialogue Ania and Piotrek are going to the cinema. Ania: Piotrek, która godzina? (Piotrek, what time is it?) Piotrek: Jest wpół do szóstej. (It's half past five.) Ania: A o której jest film? (And what time is the film?) Piotrek: O siódmej. Mamy dużo czasu. (At seven. We've got lots of time.) Ania: W sobotę też idziemy do kina? (Are we going to the cinema on Saturday too?) Piotrek: W sobotę nie mogę. Pracuję od dziewiątej do piątej. (I can't on Saturday. I work from nine to five.) Ania: A w niedzielę? (And on Sunday?) Piotrek: W niedzielę rano jadę do babci, ale wieczorem mam czas. (On Sunday morning I'm going to Grandma's, but in the evening I'm free.) Ania: Dobrze, to w niedzielę wieczorem. (OK, Sunday evening then.) Good to know The hours, 1 to 12: Hour Jest … (It's …) O … (At …) 1 pierwsza pierwszej 2 druga drugiej 3 trzecia trzeciej 4 czwarta czwartej 5 piąta piątej 6 szósta szóstej 7 siódma siódmej 8 ósma ósmej 9 dziewiąta dziewiątej 10 dziesiąta dziesiątej 11 jedenasta jedenastej 12 dwunasta dwunastej Minutes are ordinary numbers after the hour: ósma piętnaście (8:15), o ósmej czterdzieści (at 8:40). Timetables and shops use the 24-hour clock: o osiemnastej (at 18:00), o dwudziestej (at 20:00). od (from) and do (to) take the same -ej form as o: od ósmej do szesnastej. Days take w + the form you learnt in Numbers 0–100, days of the week, months : w poniedziałek, we wtorek, w środę. Culture note Polish timetables, tickets and opening hours never use \"a.m.\" and \"p.m.\": otwarte 9:00–17:00. Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Jest osiem. → ✓ Jest ósma. — hours use the \"first, second…\" form: ósma, not osiem. ✗ O ósma. → ✓ O ósmej. — after o (at) the ending changes to -ej. ✗ Jest wpół do ósmej. (for 8:30) → ✓ Jest wpół do dziewiątej. — Polish counts half an hour \"to\" the next hour. ✗ w wieczór → ✓ wieczorem — \"in the evening\" is one word. ✗ w poniedziałku → ✓ w poniedziałek — \"on Monday\" is w + the form poniedziałek. Practice Write the time in words. 3:00 — Jest . 7:00 — Jest . Complete with o + the hour. Film jest ósmej. Mam lekcję o (6:00) . Choose: rano, wieczorem, w nocy. Śpię . (at night) Oglądam film . (in the evening) Translate into Polish. What time is it? At nine. Answers trzecia siódma o szóstej w nocy wieczorem Która (jest) godzina? O dziewiątej.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-time-dates"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-home-city","group":"pages","title":"Home, furniture and places in town","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Rooms, furniture and the places you need in town: kuchnia, łazienka, łóżko, sklep, apteka, dworzec, przystanek.","text":"Most of these words are simple nouns, but two things work differently from English. First, \"in\" and \"at\" a place are not always w: you are w sklepie (in the shop) but na poczcie (at the post office), so learn each place with its little word. Second, drzwi (door) is plural only, like English \"scissors\": Drzwi są otwarte. Words Home Polish English dom (m) house, home mieszkanie (n) flat, apartment pokój (m) room kuchnia (f) kitchen łazienka (f) bathroom sypialnia (f) bedroom salon (m) living room przedpokój (m) hall balkon (m) balcony ogród (m) garden piętro (n) floor, storey winda (f) lift, elevator drzwi (plural) door okno (n) window Furniture and things Polish English stół (m) table krzesło (n) chair łóżko (n) bed szafa (f) wardrobe kanapa (f) sofa biurko (n) desk lampa (f) lamp lodówka (f) fridge półka (f) shelf Places in town Polish English sklep (m) shop apteka (f) pharmacy, chemist's dworzec (m) (railway or bus) station przystanek (m) (bus or tram) stop poczta (f) post office bank (m) bank szkoła (f) school szpital (m) hospital kościół (m) church park (m) park ulica (f) street restauracja (f) restaurant kawiarnia (f) cafe kino (n) cinema muzeum (n) museum hotel (m) hotel lotnisko (n) airport rynek (m) market square centrum (n) (town) centre Word partners Polish English Mam małe mieszkanie i duży balkon. I have a small flat and a big balcony. Mieszkanie ma dwa pokoje. The flat has two rooms. Gdzie jest apteka? Where's the pharmacy? Przystanek jest blisko. The stop is near. Dworzec jest daleko. The station is far away. W bloku nie ma windy. There's no lift in the block of flats. Idę do sklepu. I'm going to the shop. Jadę na dworzec. I'm going to the station. Mieszkam w centrum. I live in the centre. Jestem w domu. I'm at home. Good to know w or na? Say w sklepie, w banku, w szkole, w kinie, but na poczcie, na dworcu, na przystanku, na rynku. Learn them as phrases for now; the rule comes with Locative: saying where you are . parter is the ground floor, and pierwsze piętro is the floor above it (British \"first floor\", American \"second floor\"). pokój also means \"peace\". blok is a block of flats. dworzec is a station building for trains or buses; przystanek is a stop in the street. Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Jestem w poczcie. → ✓ Jestem na poczcie. — some places take na, not w. ✗ Czekam w przystanku. → ✓ Czekam na przystanku. — most places take w; learn the na-places as a list: poczta, dworzec, przystanek, rynek. ✗ Drzwi jest otwarte. → ✓ Drzwi są otwarte. — drzwi is plural only. ✗ Gdzie jest stacja autobusowa? (for a bus stop in the street) → ✓ Gdzie jest przystanek? — a stop is przystanek. ✗ Mam duży mieszkanie. → ✓ Mam duże mieszkanie. — mieszkanie is neuter: -e. Practice Write the gender: (m), (f) or (n). łóżko kuchnia stół Which room? You sleep in the . You cook in the . w or na? Jestem poczcie. Translate into Polish. Where's the stop? The flat has a balcony. Answers (n) (f) (m) sypialnia kuchnia na Gdzie jest przystanek? Mieszkanie ma balkon.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-home-city"},{"id":"page:a1-cases-locative-places","group":"pages","title":"Locative: saying where you are","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"w Krakowie, w domu, na uniwersytecie, w pracy, w Polsce","level":"A1","category":"Cases","route":"#/a1/cases-locative-places"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-going-isc-jechac","group":"pages","title":"Going places: iść and jechać","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"Idę do sklepu. Jadę do Warszawy. Idę na koncert.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: aspect and tenses","route":"#/a1/verbs-going-isc-jechac"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-possessives-demonstratives","group":"pages","title":"Possessives and demonstratives","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"mój, twój, jego, jej, nasz, wasz, ich; ten, ta, to","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-possessives-demonstratives"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-past-tense-basics","group":"pages","title":"Past tense: first forms","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"byłem / byłam, miałem / miałam, robiłem / robiłam","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: aspect and tenses","route":"#/a1/verbs-past-tense-basics"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-asking-to-repeat","group":"pages","title":"Survival phrases: ask people to repeat, slow down, explain","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"Proszę powtórzyć. Proszę mówić wolniej. Nie rozumiem. Jak to się mówi po polsku?","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-asking-to-repeat"},{"id":"cards","group":"exercises","title":"Flashcards and mistakes","headings":[],"summary":"One review queue: flashcards from decks, My mistakes and weak spots from practice, at growing intervals.","text":"flashcards review active recall decks mistake log weak spots","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review"},{"id":"cards:mistakes","group":"exercises","title":"My mistakes (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"A flashcard deck made from the entries of the mistake log.","text":"flashcards my mistakes mistake log","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/u-mistakes"},{"id":"cards:words","group":"exercises","title":"My words","headings":[],"summary":"Words and phrases you selected in texts, with the sentence and the meaning.","text":"my words personal glossary saved words flashcards selection","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/words"},{"id":"cards:deck:case-accusative","group":"exercises","title":"Accusative in sentences (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Sample of 6 cards. The full deck has 30 cards. Put the phrase in brackets into the accusative.","text":"flashcards deck Accusative in sentences A1-A2","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/case-accusative"},{"id":"set:a1-cases-accusative-1","group":"exercises","title":"Accusative: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: choose the form, fill the gaps, find the mistake and translate.","text":"Topic: Accusative: the direct object","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-cases-accusative-1"},{"id":"set:a1-cases-accusative-2","group":"exercises","title":"Accusative: type it yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: type the forms, write a dictation, rewrite sentences, find the mistake and translate.","text":"Topic: Accusative: the direct object","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-cases-accusative-2"},{"id":"set:a1-cases-accusative-3","group":"exercises","title":"Accusative: listen, rewrite, fill the table","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: two dictations, short answers, rewriting, a table of forms, pairs and a translation.","text":"Topic: Accusative: the direct object","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-cases-accusative-3"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation-1","group":"exercises","title":"Sounds and stress: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: find the stress, match letters and sounds, add the marks and write a dictation.","text":"Topic: Alphabet, sounds and stress","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-pronouns-pan-pani-1","group":"exercises","title":"Pronouns and pan / pani: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: choose the pronoun, fill the table, pick the polite form and correct the mistakes.","text":"Topic: Personal pronouns and formal address","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-pronouns-pan-pani-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-byc-1","group":"exercises","title":"być: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: conjugate być, choose to jest or to są, correct the mistakes and translate.","text":"Topic: być (to be) and to jest / to są","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-byc-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-gender-of-nouns-1","group":"exercises","title":"Gender of nouns: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: find the gender, choose ten, ta or to, correct the mistakes and translate.","text":"Topic: The three genders of nouns","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-gender-of-nouns-1"},{"id":"set:a1-cases-nominative-1","group":"exercises","title":"Nominative: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: make adjectives agree, ask kto or co, correct the mistakes and translate.","text":"Topic: Nominative: naming things; adjective agreement","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-cases-nominative-1"},{"id":"set:a1-cases-instrumental-identity-1","group":"exercises","title":"Instrumental: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: jobs and nationalities after być, z meaning 'with', mistakes and translations.","text":"Topic: Instrumental after być and with z","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-cases-instrumental-identity-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-present-conjugations-1","group":"exercises","title":"Present tense: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the three patterns, -ować verbs, mistakes and translations.","text":"Topic: Present tense: the three conjugation patterns","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-present-conjugations-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-common-irregular-1","group":"exercises","title":"Irregular verbs: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: mieć, jeść, wiedzieć, iść, jechać, chcieć and móc.","text":"Topic: mieć and other common irregular verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-common-irregular-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-questions-negation-1","group":"exercises","title":"Questions and negation: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: czy, question words, nie and double negation.","text":"Topic: Questions and negation","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-questions-negation-1"},{"id":"set:a1-cases-genitive-basics-1","group":"exercises","title":"Genitive: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: nie + object, nie ma, \"of\", and do, z, od, bez, dla.","text":"Topic: Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-cases-genitive-basics-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-modal-verbs-basics-1","group":"exercises","title":"Like, want, can, must: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: lubić, chcieć, móc, musieć, umieć + infinitive.","text":"Topic: lubić, chcieć, móc, musieć, umieć + infinitive","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-modal-verbs-basics-1"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u1","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Sounds and first words","headings":[],"summary":"Sounds and stress, pronouns and pan / pani, and być, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Alphabet, sounds and stress, Personal pronouns and formal address, być (to be) and to jest / to są","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u1"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u2","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: People and things","headings":[],"summary":"Gender, the nominative with adjectives, and the instrumental after być and z, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: The three genders of nouns, Nominative: naming things; adjective agreement, Instrumental after być and with z","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u2"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u3","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Doing things","headings":[],"summary":"The present tense, irregular verbs, the accusative, and questions and negation, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Present tense: the three conjugation patterns, mieć and other common irregular verbs, Accusative: the direct object, Questions and negation","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u3"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u4","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Likes and needs","headings":[],"summary":"The genitive and lubić, chcieć, móc, musieć, umieć + infinitive, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions, lubić, chcieć, móc, musieć, umieć + infinitive","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u4"},{"id":"lab","group":"lab","title":"Case Lab","headings":[],"summary":"Seven Polish cases on cards, Pick the case, Compare, Declension and the aspect strip.","text":"cases case lab declension endings nominative genitive dative accusative instrumental locative vocative aspect","level":null,"category":"Case Lab","route":"#/lab"},{"id":"lab:nominative","group":"lab","title":"Nominative (Mianownik)","headings":["Subject","After to jest / to są","Adjective after być","Name after nazywać się"],"summary":"The dictionary form: the subject of the sentence, and the noun after to jest / to są.","text":"kto? co? Anna pracuje w banku. Anna works at a bank. To jest mój brat. This is my brother. Kawa jest gorąca. The coffee is hot. Nazywam się Tomek. My name is Tomek. być + adjective nazywać się to jest to są After być a noun goes into the instrumental: Jestem studentem, not Jestem student. With to jest the noun stays in the nominative: To jest student. Masculine personal plurals change the last consonant: student → studenci, Polak → Polacy. Not studenty, Polaki. The subject is in the nominative. The direct object is in the accusative: feminine nouns change -a to -ę. Kawa jest dobra. Lubię kawę.","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · case card","route":"#/lab/nominative"},{"id":"lab:genitive","group":"lab","title":"Genitive (Dopełniacz)","headings":["After negation","Possession (of whom?)","Quantity and containers","After do, z, bez, dla, u"],"summary":"Absence, possession, quantity, and after many prepositions.","text":"kogo? czego? Nie mam czasu. I don't have time. To jest samochód Marka. This is Marek's car. Poproszę butelkę wody. A bottle of water, please. Idę do sklepu. I'm going to the shop. do z (from) od bez dla u obok koło szukać słuchać używać bać się potrzebować nie ma nie mam, nie lubię … dużo mało kilka pięć i więcej English does not change the object after not, so learners keep the accusative: Nie mam czas ✗ → Nie mam czasu ✓. Masculine people and animals that end in a consonant always take -a (brata, kota). For things it is -a or -u, and you learn them in pairs (dom – domu, chleb – chleba). After nie only an object that would be in the accusative changes. The subject stays in the nominative (Kawa nie jest droga), and other cases stay too (Nie pomagam mamie). A positive sentence takes the accusative. The same sentence with nie takes the genitive. Mam samochód. Nie mam samochodu. do + genitive = where to (a shop, a city). w + locative = where you are. Jadę do Krakowa. Mieszkam w Krakowie.","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · case card","route":"#/lab/genitive"},{"id":"lab:accusative","group":"lab","title":"Accusative (Biernik)","headings":["Direct object","Ordering","Where to, after na","Days of the week"],"summary":"The direct object: the person or thing the action goes to.","text":"kogo? co? Czytam książkę. I'm reading a book. Poproszę kawę. A coffee, please. Idę na koncert. I'm going to a concert. w sobotę on Saturday na (where to) w (days: w sobotę) przez za (in … time) o (prosić o, pytać o) mieć lubić znać widzieć czytać pić jeść kupować kochać odwiedzać English marks the object only by its place in the sentence. Polish needs the ending: Lubię kawę, not Lubię kawa. Masculine people and animals take -a, like the genitive: Mam brata, Mam psa. Things do not change: Mam dom. After nie the object switches to the genitive: Mam czas. → Nie mam czasu. Going into a building or a town takes do + genitive, not w + accusative: Idę do szkoły, not Idę w szkołę. Positive sentence: accusative. Negated sentence: genitive. Mam samochód. Nie mam samochodu. A feminine noun ends in -a as the subject and in -ę as the object. Książka jest ciekawa. Czytam książkę. na + accusative = where to. na + locative = where. With w, where to is usually do + genitive: Idę do szkoły, Jestem w szkole. Idę na pocztę. Jestem na poczcie.","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · case card","route":"#/lab/accusative"},{"id":"lab:model:nowy-student","group":"lab","title":"nowy student – all forms","headings":[],"summary":"new student, masculine personal: 14 forms in the declension viewer.","text":"declension nowy student nowego studenta nowemu studentowi nowym studentem nowym studencie nowy studencie nowi studenci nowych studentów nowym studentom nowymi studentami nowych studentach","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · declension","route":"#/lab/forms/model/nowy-student"},{"id":"lab:model:stary-dom","group":"lab","title":"stary dom – all forms","headings":[],"summary":"old house, masculine inanimate: 14 forms in the declension viewer.","text":"declension stary dom starego domu staremu domowi starym domem starym domu stary domu stare domy starych domów starym domom starymi domami starych domach","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · declension","route":"#/lab/forms/model/stary-dom"},{"id":"lab:model:dobra-kawa","group":"lab","title":"dobra kawa – all forms","headings":[],"summary":"good coffee, feminine: 14 forms in the declension viewer.","text":"declension dobra kawa dobrej kawy dobrej kawie dobrą kawę dobrą kawą dobra kawo dobre kawy dobrych kaw dobrym kawom dobrymi kawami dobrych kawach","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · declension","route":"#/lab/forms/model/dobra-kawa"},{"id":"lab:model:male-okno","group":"lab","title":"małe okno – all forms","headings":[],"summary":"small window, neuter: 14 forms in the declension viewer.","text":"declension małe okno małego okna małemu oknu małym oknem małym oknie małe okna małych okien małym oknom małymi oknami małych oknach","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · declension","route":"#/lab/forms/model/male-okno"},{"id":"lab:tree:pick-case","group":"lab","title":"Pick the case","headings":[],"summary":"Think of one noun in your sentence and answer the questions about it.","text":"Is there a preposition right before the noun (or before its adjective)? Yes, there is a preposition   No preposition   Which preposition is it? do, od, bez, dla, u, obok, koło These prepositions always take the genitive. Idę do sklepu. z   na   w   przez, or za meaning in … time (za tydzień) przez and za meaning in … time take the accusative. (za meaning behind takes the instrumental: za domem.) Wracam za tydzień. o, po, przy (about, after, next to) o, po and przy usually take the locative: mówić o filmie, po lekcji, przy oknie. (o meaning for, as in prosić o, takes the accusative, and so does po meaning to fetch: Idę po chleb.) Rozmawiamy o pracy. przed, pod, nad, między (in front of, under, above, between) When they say where something is, these prepositions take the instrumental. (Going under or in front of something takes the accusative: Kot wchodzi pod stół.) Czekam przed domem. What does z mean here? from, out of (Where from?) z meaning from takes the genitive. Jestem z Polski. with, together with (With whom? With what?) z meaning with takes the instrumental. Piję kawę z mlekiem. Does na say where something goes, or where it is? Where it goes (Where to?) na + accusative = where to. Idę na koncert. Where it is (Where?) na + locative = where. Jestem na koncercie. What does w say here? Where something is (Where?) w + locative = where: in a building, a town, a country. Mieszkam w Krakowie. Where someone goes: into a building, a town, a country Going into a place usually takes do + genitive, not w: Idę do szkoły, not Idę w szkołę. Jadę do Warszawy. A day of the week (on Monday …) w + a day of the week takes the accusative. (Months and years take the locative: w maju.) Do zobaczenia w sobotę. A month or a year (in May, in 2025) w + a month or a year takes the locative. Mam urlop w maju. What does the noun do in the sentence? It is the subject: the person or thing that does the action The subject is in the nominative. (After quantity words like dużo or pięć it goes into the genitive: Pięć osób czeka.) Mój brat pracuje w Warszawie. It comes after to jest or to są After to jest / to są the noun stays in the nominative. To jest moja siostra. It is a noun after być saying who someone is (a job, a nationality, a role) być + noun takes the instrumental: Jestem lekarzem. With a first name in an introduction the nominative is usual: Jestem Tomek. An adjective after być stays in the nominative: Kawa jest gorąca. Ona jest nauczycielką. It is the object of the verb: the person or thing the action goes to   It says whose something is, or follows a quantity word (dużo, mało, pięć …) Possession and quantities take the genitive. Mam dużo pracy. It is the person who receives something (To whom?) The receiver of a gift, a message or help is in the dative. Daję mamie kwiaty. It is a tool or a means of transport (With what? By what?) Tools and means of transport without a preposition take the instrumental. Jadę autobusem. You are speaking to someone directly Addressing someone takes the vocative. Dzień dobry, panie profesorze! Which kind of verb is it? szukać, słuchać, potrzebować, używać, bać się These verbs take the genitive, also in a positive sentence. Szukam klucza. pomagać, dziękować, or the person after dawać (komu?) The person you help, thank or give something to is in the dative, also after nie. The thing you give stays in the accusative: Daję mamie kwiaty. Pomagam bratu. Another verb (mieć, lubić, kupować, czytać …)   Is the verb negated with nie? Yes, there is nie before the verb After nie the direct object switches from the accusative to the genitive. Nie lubię kawy. No, the sentence is positive Most verbs take a direct object in the accusative. Kupuję gazetę i kawę.","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · decision tree","route":"#/lab/pick/pick-case"},{"id":"lab:aspect","group":"lab","title":"Aspect and tense on one timeline","headings":[],"summary":"Most Polish verbs come in pairs. The imperfective verb looks at the action as a process or a habit; the perfective verb looks at one finished result. Together they give five forms.","text":"aspect imperfective perfective tense czytać przeczytać Present imperfective czyta Ola czyta książkę. Ola is reading a book. Past imperfective czytała Wczoraj Ola czytała książkę przez dwie godziny. Yesterday Ola spent two hours reading a book. Past perfective przeczytała Ola już przeczytała tę książkę. Ola has already read this book. Future imperfective będzie czytać Jutro Ola będzie czytać cały wieczór. Tomorrow Ola will be reading all evening. Future perfective przeczyta Ola przeczyta tę książkę do piątku. Ola will have read this book by Friday.","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · aspect","route":"#/lab/aspect"}]};
